  "content": {
    "id": "p41-25j-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "A fair coin is repeatedly flipped.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p41-25j-q11a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the probability that $n$ consecutive heads eventually occur.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1$.\n\nIndependent blocks of $n$ flips eventually contain an all-heads block with probability $1$.",
          "worked": "Divide the flips into disjoint blocks of $n$. Each block consists entirely of heads with probability $2^{-n}$, independently of the other blocks. Let $E$ be the event that $n$ consecutive heads eventually occur. The probability that an all-heads block occurs is\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP(\\text{an all-heads block occurs})&=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}(1-2^{-n})^j2^{-n}\\\\\nP(\\text{an all-heads block occurs})&=\\frac{2^{-n}}{1-(1-2^{-n})}\\\\\nP(\\text{an all-heads block occurs})&=1.\n\\end{align*}$$\nAn all-heads block guarantees $E$. Hence $P(E)=1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p41-25j-q11b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the probability that $n$ consecutive heads occur before $m$ consecutive tails.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{2^m-1}{2^m+2^n-2}$.\n\nCondition on the first flip and solve the equations for a run starting with a head or a tail.",
          "worked": "Let $p$ be the probability of heads winning given that the first flip is a head, and $q$ the probability given that it is a tail. After the first head, either the next $n-1$ flips are heads, or a tail interrupts that run and the probability becomes $q$. After the first tail, heads can win only if a head interrupts the next $m-1$ tails. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}\np&=2^{-(n-1)}+(1-2^{-(n-1)})q,\\\\\nq&=(1-2^{-(m-1)})p.\n\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting the second equation into the first gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\np&=2^{-(n-1)}+(1-2^{-(n-1)})(1-2^{-(m-1)})p,\\\\\np(2^{m-1}+2^{n-1}-1)&=2^{m-1},\\\\\np&=\\frac{2^{m-1}}{2^{m-1}+2^{n-1}-1},\\\\\nq&=\\frac{2^{m-1}-1}{2^{m-1}+2^{n-1}-1}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nEach first-flip outcome has probability $\\tfrac12$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP(\\text{heads wins})&=\\frac12p+\\frac12q\\\\\nP(\\text{heads wins})&=\\frac{2^m-1}{2^m+2^n-2}.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      }
    ]
  },
